{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AhBNLnrPqhzF2C71d4zShM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"\n2\tConstraint extractor: extract structured math constraints from LLM reasoning traces.\n3\tInput: reasoning trace string\n4\tOutput: list of constraint dicts with type, expression, z3_formula, source_step\n5\tDependencies: json, re, openai (optional)\n6\t\"\"\"\n7\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n8\t\n9\timport asyncio\n10\timport json\n11\timport logging\n12\timport re\n13\tfrom abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n14\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n15\t\n16\tlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n17\t\n18\tEXTRACTION_PROMPT = '''You are a mathematical constraint extractor. Given a step-by-step reasoning trace for a math problem, extract all mathematical constraints (equations, inequalities, predicates) that appear in the reasoning.\n19\t\n20\t**Variable naming rules:**\n21\t- Use standard variable names: x, y, z, a, b, c, n, m, k\n22\t- If the problem names specific quantities (e.g., \"length\", \"width\"), use descriptive short names (e.g., l, w)\n23\t- Be consistent: the same quantity must use the same variable name across all constraints\n24\t- Use the SAME variable names that appear in the reasoning trace\n25\t\n26\t**Output format:** Return a JSON object with this exact structure:\n27\t```json\n28\t{{\n29\t  \"constraints\": [\n30\t    {{\n31\t      \"type\": \"equation|inequality|predicate\",\n32\t      \"expression\": \"human-readable expression, e.g. x + y == 14\",\n33\t      \"z3_formula\": \"Z3-compatible Python expression, e.g. x + y == 14\",\n34\t      \"source_step\": 3\n35\t    }}\n36\t  ],\n37\t  \"answer\": \"the final numerical answer from this trace\",\n38\t  \"variables\": [\"x\", \"y\"]\n39\t}}\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\t**Constraint types:**\n43\t- equation: equality (==), e.g. \"x + y == 14\"\n44\t- inequality: <, >, <=, >=, e.g. \"x > 0\"\n45\t- predicate: logical assertion, e.g. \"x % 2 == 0\" (x is even)\n46\t\n47\t**Z3 formula rules:**\n48\t- Use Python syntax: ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=, +, -, *, /, **\n49\t- Division: use / (Z3 Real division)\n50\t- Integer modulo: use %\n51\t- All variables are Real by default\n52\t\n53\t**Example 1:**\n54\tTrace: \"Let the two numbers be x and y. We know x + y = 14 and x * y = 48. From x + y = 14, y = 14 - x. Substituting: x(14-x) = 48, 14x - x^2 = 48, x^2 - 14x + 48 = 0. Factoring: (x-6)(x-8) = 0. So x = 6 or x = 8. The answer is \\\\\\\\boxed{{6}}.\"\n55\t\n56\tOutput:\n57\t```json\n58\t{{\n59\t  \"constraints\": [\n60\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"x + y == 14\", \"z3_formula\": \"x + y == 14\", \"source_step\": 1}},\n61\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"x * y == 48\", \"z3_formula\": \"x * y == 48\", \"source_step\": 1}},\n62\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"y == 14 - x\", \"z3_formula\": \"y == 14 - x\", \"source_step\": 2}},\n63\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"x**2 - 14*x + 48 == 0\", \"z3_formula\": \"x**2 - 14*x + 48 == 0\", \"source_step\": 3}}\n64\t  ],\n65\t  \"answer\": \"6\",\n66\t  \"variables\": [\"x\", \"y\"]\n67\t}}\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t**Example 2:**\n71\tTrace: \"A rectangle has perimeter 30 and area 50. Let length = l, width = w. Then 2(l+w) = 30, so l+w = 15. Also l*w = 50. We need l - w. (l-w)^2 = (l+w)^2 - 4lw = 225 - 200 = 25. So l-w = 5. The answer is \\\\\\\\boxed{{5}}.\"\n72\t\n73\tOutput:\n74\t```json\n75\t{{\n76\t  \"constraints\": [\n77\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"2*(l + w) == 30\", \"z3_formula\": \"2*(l + w) == 30\", \"source_step\": 1}},\n78\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"l + w == 15\", \"z3_formula\": \"l + w == 15\", \"source_step\": 2}},\n79\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"l * w == 50\", \"z3_formula\": \"l * w == 50\", \"source_step\": 2}},\n80\t    {{\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"(l - w)**2 == 25\", \"z3_formula\": \"(l - w)**2 == 25\", \"source_step\": 3}}\n81\t  ],\n82\t  \"answer\": \"5\",\n83\t  \"variables\": [\"l\", \"w\"]\n84\t}}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tNow extract constraints from this trace:\n88\t\n89\t{trace}\n90\t\n91\tReturn ONLY the JSON object, no other text.'''\n92\t\n93\t\n94\tclass LLMBackend(ABC):\n95\t    \"\"\"LLM 调用抽象基类\"\"\"\n96\t    @abstractmethod\n97\t    def call(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n98\t        ...\n99\t\n100\t\n101\tclass VLLMBackend(LLMBackend):\n102\t    \"\"\"通过 OpenAI-compatible API 调用本地 vLLM\"\"\"\n103\t\n104\t    def __init__(self, base_url: str = \"http://localhost:8000/v1\", model: str | None = None):\n105\t        from openai import OpenAI, AsyncOpenAI\n106\t        self.client = OpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=[REDACTED]"EMPTY\")\n107\t        self.async_client = AsyncOpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=[REDACTED]"EMPTY\")\n108\t        if model is None:\n109\t            models = self.client.models.list()\n110\t            self.model = models.data[0].id\n111\t        else:\n112\t            self.model = model\n113\t\n114\t    def call(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n115\t        response = self.client.chat.completions.create(\n116\t            model=self.model,\n117\t            messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n118\t            temperature=0.3,\n119\t            max_tokens=2048,\n120\t        )\n121\t        return response.choices[0].message.content or \"\"\n122\t\n123\t    async def async_call(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n124\t        response = await self.async_client.chat.completions.create(\n125\t            model=self.model,\n126\t            messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n127\t            temperature=0.3,\n128\t            max_tokens=2048,\n129\t        )\n130\t        return response.choices[0].message.content or \"\"\n131\t\n132\t    def batch_call(self, prompts: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n133\t        \"\"\"批量调用，用 asyncio 并发\"\"\"\n134\t        async def _run():\n135\t            tasks = [self.async_call(p) for p in prompts]\n136\t            return await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n137\t        try:\n138\t            loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n139\t            import nest_asyncio\n140\t            nest_asyncio.apply()\n141\t            return loop.run_until_complete(_run())\n142\t        except RuntimeError:\n143\t            return asyncio.run(_run())\n144\t\n145\t\n146\tclass MockLLM(LLMBackend):\n147\t    \"\"\"Returns pre-set responses for testing.\"\"\"\n148\t\n149\t    def __init__(self, responses: list[str] | None = None):\n150\t        self._responses = responses or []\n151\t        self._idx = 0\n152\t\n153\t    def call(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n154\t        if self._responses:\n155\t            resp = self._responses[self._idx % len(self._responses)]\n156\t            self._idx += 1\n157\t            return resp\n158\t        return json.dumps({\"constraints\": [\n159\t            {\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"x - 7\", \"z3_formula\": \"x == 7\", \"source_step\": 1},\n160\t            {\"type\": \"equation\", \"expression\": \"x + 5 - 12\", \"z3_formula\": \"x + 5 == 12\", \"source_step\": 2},\n161\t        ], \"answer\": \"7\", \"variables\": [\"x\"]})\n162\t\n163\t\n164\tclass APIBasedLLM(LLMBackend):\n165\t    \"\"\"LLM backend using OpenAI-compatible API (e.g. vLLM server).\"\"\"\n166\t\n167\t    def __init__(self, base_url: str = \"http://localhost:8000/v1\",\n168\t                 api_key: [REDACTED] = \"EMPTY\", model: str | None = None,\n169\t                 temperature: float = 0.1, max_tokens: int = 2048):\n170\t        import openai\n171\t        self.client = openai.OpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=[REDACTED]        self.temperature = temperature\n173\t        self.max_tokens = max_tokens\n174\t        if model:\n175\t            self.model = model\n176\t        else:\n177\t            models = self.client.models.list()\n178\t            self.model = models.data[0].id\n179\t\n180\t    def call(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n181\t        resp = self.client.chat.completions.create(\n182\t            model=self.model,\n183\t            messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n184\t            temperature=self.temperature,\n185\t            max_tokens=self.max_tokens,\n186\t        )\n187\t        return resp.choices[0].message.content or \"\"\n188\t\n189\t\n190\t@dataclass\n191\tclass ExtractionStats:\n192\t    success: int = 0\n193\t    fail_json_parse: int = 0\n194\t    fail_empty: int = 0\n195\t    fail_invalid_expr: int = 0\n196\t\n197\t    @property\n198\t    def total_fail(self) -> int:\n199\t        return self.fail_json_parse + self.fail_empty + self.fail_invalid_expr\n200\t\n201\t\n202\tclass ConstraintExtractor:\n203\t    def __init__(self, llm: LLMBackend | None = None):\n204\t        self.llm = llm or MockLLM()\n205\t        self.stats = ExtractionStats()\n206\t\n207\t    def _build_prompt(self, trace: str) -> str:\n208\t        return EXTRACTION_PROMPT.format(trace=trace)\n209\t\n210\t    def _parse_response(self, raw: str, trace: str) -> list[dict]:\n211\t        \"\"\"Parse a raw LLM response into constraint list.\"\"\"\n212\t        json_str = raw.strip()\n213\t        m = re.search(r'```(?:json)?\\s*(.*?)```', json_str, re.DOTALL)\n214\t        if m:\n215\t            json_str = m.group(1).strip()\n216\t\n217\t        try:\n218\t            data = json.loads(json_str)\n219\t        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n220\t            logger.warning(\"JSON parse failed for trace: %s...\", trace[:80])\n221\t            self.stats.fail_json_parse += 1\n222\t            return []\n223\t\n224\t        constraints = data.get(\"constraints\", [])\n225\t        if not constraints:\n226\t            self.stats.fail_empty += 1\n227\t            return []\n228\t\n229\t        valid = []\n230\t        for c in constraints:\n231\t            if not all(k in c for k in (\"type\", \"expression\", \"z3_formula\", \"source_step\")):\n232\t                continue\n233\t            if c[\"type\"] not in (\"equation\", \"inequality\", \"predicate\"):\n234\t                continue\n235\t            valid.append(c)\n236\t\n237\t        if not valid:\n238\t            self.stats.fail_invalid_expr += 1\n239\t            return []\n240\t\n241\t        self.stats.success += 1\n242\t        return valid\n243\t\n244\t    def extract(self, trace: str) -> list[dict]:\n245\t        \"\"\"Extract structured constraints from a reasoning trace.\"\"\"\n246\t        prompt = self._build_prompt(trace)\n247\t        raw = self.llm.call(prompt)\n248\t        return self._parse_response(raw, trace)\n249\t\n250\t    def extract_batch(self, traces: list[str]) -> list[list[dict]]:\n251\t        \"\"\"批量提取约束，如果 LLM 后端支持 batch_call 则并发执行\"\"\"\n252\t        if hasattr(self.llm, 'batch_call'):\n253\t            prompts = [self._build_prompt(t) for t in traces]\n254\t            raw_results = self.llm.batch_call(prompts)\n255\t            return [self._parse_response(raw, trace)\n256\t                    for raw, trace in zip(raw_results, traces)]\n257\t        return [self.extract(t) for t in traces]\n258\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTTtHKEDzmwvFFpx5YDYWD","session_id":"78f4268f-add9-4f5f-97a8-4d696a1c2e4e","uuid":"8c1b28ca-5262-47de-bbde-1ac49d79af76","timestamp":"2026-05-16T06:20:07.485Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E9Wj7qkarKs5RajkoSVQ6c","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_3/projects/symb_invariant_consensus/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map — symb_invariant_consensus (SICA)\\\\n# Last updated: 2026-05-16\\\\n# Purpose: Track related work coverage for EMNLP 2026 submission\\\\n\\\\ndimensions:\\\\n\\\\n  self_consistency_and_variants:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Self-Consistency (SC) decoding and improvements\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: wang2023sc\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2203.11171\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Foundational SC method; our primary baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: chen2024usc\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Universal Self-Consistency for Large Language Model Generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Chen et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2311.17311\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Extends SC to free-form answers via LLM-based selection; alternative aggregation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: taubenfeld2025cisc\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Confidence Improves Self-Consistency in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Taubenfeld et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ACL Findings 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2502.06233\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Weighted voting via model confidence; reduces samples 40-50%. Shows SC can be improved with non-uniform weighting — SICA uses constraint-based weighting instead\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: kim2026reasc\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Reliability-Aware Adaptive Self-Consistency (ReASC)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Kim et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Response-level confidence for early halting; 70-80% cost saving vs naive SC\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: loo2025reevaluating\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Reevaluating Self-Consistency Scaling in Multi-Agent Systems\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Loo et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2511.00751\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"CRITICAL — shows SC gains plateau early and can decline at high sample counts for strong models. Directly supports our QwQ-32B finding (SICA≡SC when model is too consistent)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: wan2024rasc\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Reasoning-Aware Self-Consistency (RASC)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Wan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Combines answer-level and rationale-level features; reduces samples ~88%\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: dsc2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Difficulty-Adaptive Self-Consistency\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NAACL Findings 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Ranks problem difficulty to allocate sampling budget; complementary efficiency approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n  symbolic_reasoning_llm:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Neuro-symbolic methods combining LLMs with formal solvers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: pan2023logiclm\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Logic-LM: Empowering Large Language Models with Symbolic Solvers for Faithful Logical Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Pan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP Findings 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2305.12295\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"LLM→FOL→solver pipeline; closest architectural ancestor to SICA. Key difference: Logic-LM uses single-path translation, SICA aggregates across multiple paths\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: xu2026dylos\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Adaptive LLM-Symbolic Reasoning via Dynamic Logical Solver Composition (DyLoS)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Xu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"EACL 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.06774\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Multi-paradigm solver selection (LP/FOL/CSP/SMT); evaluated on FOLIO+ProofWriter. Outperforms Logic-LM. Key difference: DyLoS selects solver type, SICA aggregates constraints across paths\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: fover2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"FoVer: First-Order Logic Verification for Natural Language Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"TACL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"FOL verification for NL reasoning; strong results on ProofWriter+FOLIO. Uses verification rather than aggregation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: li2025survey\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"A Survey on LLM Symbolic Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2025 (under review)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Comprehensive survey; positions SICA within the broader landscape\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: constraintllm2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"ConstraintLLM: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Industrial Constraint Programming\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Multi-instruction SFT for constraint extraction; different domain (industrial) but related extraction methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n  maxsat_constraint_nlp:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"MAX-SAT and constraint satisfaction applied to NLP/reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: llm_meets_cp2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Large Language Model Meets Constraint Propagation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"IJCAI 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"LLM+constraint propagation integration; validates feasibility of constraint-based reasoning with LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: satbench2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"SATBench: Benchmarking LLMs' Logical Reasoning via SAT Problems\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Evaluates LLMs on SAT-formulated problems; establishes that LLMs struggle with pure SAT but can benefit from solver integration\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: maxsat_local_search2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Optimizing Local Search-based Partial MaxSAT Solving via Initial Assignment Prediction\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"Science China Information Sciences 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Solver-side advances in partial MaxSAT; technical reference for our WPMS formulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n  folio_proofwriter_sota:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"State-of-the-art methods on FOLIO and ProofWriter benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: logicagent2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"From Ambiguity to Verdict: A Semiotic-Grounded Multi-Perspective Agent for LLM Logical Reasoning (LogicAgent)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2509.24765\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Multi-perspective logical reasoning; SOTA on logical reasoning benchmarks including FOLIO. +7.05% average gain\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: aristotle2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Aristotle: Mastering Logical Reasoning with A Logic-Complete Decompose-Search-Resolve Framework\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Logic-complete decomposition framework; strong ProofWriter/FOLIO results\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: qi2025symbolic_provers\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Large Language Models Meet Symbolic Provers for Logical Reasoning Evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Qi et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2502.06563\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"LLM+symbolic prover combination; evaluated on FOLIO\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: dissecting2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Dissecting Logical Reasoning in LLMs: A Fine-Grained Evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP Findings 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2506.04810\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Fine-grained analysis of LLM logical reasoning capabilities on ProofWriter/FOLIO\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n  reasoning_model_diversity:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Trace diversity in reasoning vs non-reasoning models; supports our QwQ-32B finding\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: loo2025reevaluating\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Reevaluating Self-Consistency Scaling in Multi-Agent Systems\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Loo et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2511.00751\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"CRITICAL — strong models produce near-identical paths, SC gains plateau/decline. Direct support for SICA≡SC on QwQ-32B\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: diversity_aware_tts2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Diversity-Aware Training for Test-Time Scaling\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"Under review 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Independent reasoning attempts collapse into similar solutions; diversity training needed to unlock test-time scaling. Supports our claim that non-reasoning models have more useful diversity\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: snell2025scaling_tts\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally Can be More Effective than Scaling Parameters\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Snell et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025 (Oral)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2408.03314\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Foundational test-time compute scaling; shows compute-optimal strategies vary by difficulty. Contextualizes why SICA needs diverse paths\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: deepseek_r1_2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"DeepSeek-AI\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"Nature 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2501.12948\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Defines the reasoning model paradigm (RL-trained extended thinking); reference for why these models are highly self-consistent\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: must_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: base_models_reason2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Base Models Know How to Reason, Thinking Models Learn When\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.07364\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"RL teaches activation timing not new skills; implies reasoning models converge to similar reasoning patterns, reducing trace diversity\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: korikov2025sc_ranking\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"The Impact of LLM Self-Consistency and Reasoning Effort on Automated Scoring\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Korikov et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2604.26954\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Temperature sampling helps but ensemble size (1→7) produces no significant gains; supports diminishing returns of naive SC\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n  multi_path_aggregation:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Methods for aggregating multiple reasoning paths beyond majority voting\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: rc2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Representation Consistency for Accurate and Coherent LLM Answer Aggregation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2506.21590\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Aggregation via representation similarity; down-weights incoherent reasoning. Semantic alternative to SICA's symbolic approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: should_cite\\\\n\\\\n      - id: multi_path_plan2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Enhancing Long Chain-of-Thought Reasoning through Multi-Path Plan Aggregation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.11620\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Multi-path aggregation at plan level rather than answer level\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\n      - id: crest2025\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Improving LLMs' Reasoning with Consistency-Driven Self-Training (CREST)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NAACL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Consistency as training signal rather than inference-time aggregation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        status: optional\\\\n\\\\ngaps:\\\\n  resolved: []\\\\n  remaining:\\\\n    - dimension: \\\\\\\"reasoning_model_diversity\\\\\\\"\\\\n      gap: \\\\\\\"No published paper explicitly studies QwQ-32B trace diversity collapse under temperature sampling. Our empirical finding (3.6% multi-candidate across 83 problems) is novel but needs positioning against Loo et al. 2025 and Diversity-Aware TTS\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - dimension: \\\\\\\"maxsat_constraint_nlp\\\\\\\"\\\\n      gap: \\\\\\\"No direct precedent for weighted partial MAX-SAT as multi-path consensus mechanism in NLI. SICA's formulation appears novel — must frame clearly in Related Work\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - dimension: \\\\\\\"self_consistency_and_variants\\\\\\\"\\\\n      gap: \\\\\\\"Should verify if CRAFT (mentioned in project docs) is a real published method or internal shorthand\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 11286, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UT1eKrN3KQV3nY7LVbCMHd","session_id":"78f4268f-add9-4f5f-97a8-4d696a1c2e4e","uuid":"1df975cf-b81d-4aff-a3f1-40c22ceb8073","timestamp":"2026-05-16T06:20:07.505Z"}
